I don't know if anyone has suggested this before, but to further add to the
debate on the power of "named" weapons, how about:

Intelligent weapons with ego (a la AD&D). For those who don't know, the idea is
that very powerful weapons have an "intelligence" residing in them. This may be
very basic, or it could be godlike. The weapon also has a definite alignment,
or at least a strong motivation. It might be to slay all orcs, or kill all evil
priests. Of course, what the weapon wants may not always agree with what the
character wants. This causes conflict - the weapon attempts to exert it's will
over the wielder. If it succeeds, the unfortunate player is forced to follow
the weapons will, e.g. attacking a certain monster in preference to others,
performing deeds which the character would not normally consider etc. The
weapon does not normally return control to the wielder until it feals that it
is satisfied, or the wielder performs an enormous act of willpower. Often
weapons exert their will to control when the character is week or when the
character needs help desperately.

Intelligent weapons are vain too. They will usually insist that the player
wield them in preference to any other weapon, and certainly would not cohabit
with any other intelligent weapon. Some will insist that they be adorned with
valuable gems, or have regular "feedings".

Perhaps the weapon could force the player into a mini quest that was related to
the enemy of the weapon.

To rid yourself of the weapon, maybe another mini-quest would be involved
(crushing the weapon under the heal of a storm giant - whose cooperation would
have to be secured first, or dipping into a lava pool in the depths of hell).

I think this would make the intelligent weapons much "better". Players would
have to decide if they would go for an intelligent weapon (and suffer some of
the consequences) or play it safe and just enchant a normal weapon.

On a slightly different track, maybe the Gods could enchant the players
ordinary weapon with new powers, instead of dishing out weapons like crazy
vending machines.

I can't guarantee everything I've said about intelligent weapons is correct,
or that I've remembered everything relevant, since this is all from memory.

Comments?

